oney": 0.47}, "rating_margin": 5.44, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.42, "baseline_margin": 3.21, "analyst_adjustment": 4.79, "key_factors": ["Murkowski's cross-party endorsement of Kreiss-Tomkins signals GOP fragmentation", "Polls consistently show Kreiss-Tomkins near or above 50% of the vote", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win near 88-89%", "Divided Republican field (Taylor, Wilson, Bronson) under RCV rules"], "rationale": "The fundamentals lean Republican in an open-seat Alaska, but every recent poll shows Kreiss-Tomkins at 50% or higher, raters overwhelmingly call the race a Democratic flip, and markets price him near 88%. The Murkowski endorsement and a fractured GOP field justify moving well past the modest polling-average edge toward Likely D.", "watch": "If the Republican field consolidates behind Treg Taylor before ranked-choice tabulation, the race could tighten back into a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike Dunleavy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alaska's open governorship, left by term-limited Mike Dunleavy, has become an unexpected Democratic opportunity. Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins has outraised the Republican field, led by Treg Taylor, and recent surveys from Alaska Survey Research, the New York Times/Siena, and Data for Progress all place him at or above 50% of the vote. Senator Lisa Murkowski's endorsement of the Democrat underscores a split among Alaska Republicans, with Wilson and Bronson also competing for the party's voters.\n\nUnder ranked-choice voting, a fragmented GOP field is a serious disadvantage. Most expert raters call the race a Democratic flip, though Fox News still rates it a tossup, and prediction markets price a Kreiss-Tomkins win near 88%. The race remains winnable for Republicans if the field consolidates, but the current evidence points clearly toward a Democratic pickup.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6825, "margin": 2.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7335, "